  DEFENDING ARMY

  The attackers had the 315th Dragons, the 317th Trolls and the 318th Trolls 
in reserve.  Standing at the ready with his ships was 
Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll.  Duqaq led his navies into the fray.
  The army of Uga-Buga Makumb used magic to aid their cause.  Magical 
phantasmal navies fought with hiss soldiers.  His luck was terrible!  A 
thundercloud formed overhead and his flag squadron was drenched by a dark 
thundershower.  This bad omen completely disheartened his forces.  Also, in 
spite of his industrious planning, Uga-Buga Makumb was tactically 
outmaneuvered and saw that it was becoming a difficult battle.
  The Dwarves commanded by Duqaq were not impressed by his strange 
appearance.
  Across the entire front, the Veteran 316th Bowmen faced the 273rd Fleet 
Marines.  The 293rd Dragons soared over the 273rd Fleet Marines and attacked 
them.  The 316th Bowmen battled with the 273rd Fleet Marines and easily 
destroyed them.
  Having found a weak point in the line of the armada of Circe (#8), a final 
savage slashing attack defeated their enemy and won the battle for 
Uga-Buga Makumb.  The 316th Bowmen scattered the 273rd Fleet Marines in all 
directions.
  The Moonshine Sea was conquered by Uga-Buga Makumb's forces in a bloody 
campaign.  The battle affected the quality of our forces:  we could not have 
been better satisfied with the elan of the 314th Dragons, and we have 
elevated its rating to Veteran.  His forces completely destroyed, Duqaq 
(#50) escaped frightened and alone to the Flying Fish Sea.
  During early summer, Uga-Buga Makumb rode his flying armies into Tabora.  
To the northeast lies a Desert province called Tebessa (#704), populated by 
Humans.  It is ruled by Kwoth.  Their Human warriors array themselves in the 
style of Legion and Heavy Cavalry.  West of here lies the prosperous 
province of Jerbilia (#605), ruled by Kwoth.  It is said to be a Desert 
province inhabited by Elves.  Their Elven fighters array themselves as 
Camel Corps.  Viewing the northwestern horizon, a man can see a rich Jungle 
province called Ngara (#604).  It is said that Kwoth reigns there.  It is 
known to be populated by Humans.  Their Human warriors traditionally fight 
in the style of Legion and Light Cavalry.

  Soldiers bearing the battle-standards of the Uga-Buga Makumbian empire 
marched on the Jungle province of Tabora, a land defended by the soldiers of 
Kwoth (#3).  Uga-Buga Makumb's armies took possession of Tabora practically 
uncontested.  Tabora was found to have an income of 10 gold.
  With the coming of the late summer season, Uga-Buga Makumb recruited the 
341st Garrison and the 342nd Garrison from among the population of Tabora, 
to serve under the provincial garrison commander.
  During fall, Uga-Buga Makumb flew over to Ngara.  To the northeast there 
lies a Plain province known as Limerick (#703), a land of Orcs where Kwoth 
rules.  Their Orcish warriors traditionally fight in the style of Phalanx.  
Viewing the western horizon, one can see a Plain province called Chaco 
(#504).  It is said that Kwoth is the ruler there.  It is known to be 
populated by Elves.  Their Elven fighters traditionally fight as Phalanx and 
Heavy Cavalry.  Northwest of here lies the province of Chirfa (#603), ruled 
by Kwoth.  It is known to be a Desert province inhabited by Humans.  Their 
Human fighters fight in the style of Camel Corps.

  Soldiers bearing the battle-standards of the Uga-Buga Makumbian empire 
marched on the Jungle province of Ngara, a land defended by the forces of 
Kwoth.  Uga-Buga Makumb's forces encountered little resistance in Ngara and 
Uga-Buga Makumb there established our rule.  Ngara was found to have a tax 
base of 10 gold.
  With the advent of the early winter season, Uga-Buga Makumb levied the 6th 
Garrison and the 13th Garrison from among the people of Ngara, to serve 
under the provincial garrison commander.

    A cold chill accompanied the frighteningly quick and unexpected 
eclipsing  of the moon.    Wicked dissonant evil chants were heard by all 
who have ever  committed even the slightest of evil acts.    The powerful 
and intense stench  of the rotted corpses of grew thicker and quite 
unbearable, the stench began to die down.    Then  everything turned black.  
Within the beat of a dying goat's heart, an  intimidating voice was heard: 
"NATAS.    .    .     NATAS.    .    .     NATAS.    .    .      I am the 
Evil Overfuhrer Dietrich vconquest over good.    And to further aid you, I 
present the Sword Excalibur,  secured in combat against King Lazorik.    In 
the names of the great evil  minds before you: men like myself, Dark Prophet 
Marius, and Cupbearer  Cornelius, you must carry on the w
  If Roderoch searches a feature, any monetary treasure Roderoch captures 
will be given to you.  If Roderoch captures a magic treasure, Roderoch will 
give it to your King when he departs for Valhalla, or you can order Roderoch 
to Give it to one of your characters.  You can do anything you want with the 
magic item Roderoch came with, but the item will leave at the end of his 
stay with you.  Except for the rules given in this paragraph, Roderoch can 
generally be treated just like your other Heroes, and can be affected by any 
spell except Resurrection.  The main limitation in using Roderoch is that 
Roderoch has only enough patience to practice with you for one of the 
phase(s) Roderoch is with you.  Roderoch will leave at the end of the last 
phase Roderoch is scheduled to serve you, after all battles have been 
fought.  Roderoch can lead armies, but when Roderoch returns to Valhalla, 
the armies will normally be left leaderless in the province they are in.  If 
Roderoch has the armies in a province you don't own, they will be 
transferred to your lowest-numbered leader in the same province, or at last 
resort, the garrison commander in an adjacent province owned by you.  If no 
leader is available, the armies will be disbanded.
  Roderoch can be given orders only for the first 3 phase(s) of the turn.

  Thus endeth the adventures of Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll for this 
year.


  Uga-Buga Makumb treated with his ally Lord Babba Sissoko Master orango 
(#0).  Hokuto (#4) informed Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll that our 
former amicable relationship has been rendered meaningless by 
Uga-Buga Makumb's outrageous provocations.

  Your Majesty, I have news to report of wanderers in our kingdom:  
  Kokocala (#10) grew tired of Mare Ionium and left for Ravenwood.
  Dracona (#38) awoke one morning with a desire to see the Stonegard Sea, 
and so left the Ark Royal Sea to journey to that province.
  Pascal (#78) has arrived in Ngara from Jerbilia, drawn by the fine (or 
infamous?  ?)   reputation of its king.


  Here is the financial report for Uga-Buga Makumb's kingdom: this turn, the 
Kingdom collected 138 gold in revenue.  26 gold was expended to finance our 
leaders.  The 6th Garrison, the 13th Garrison, the 341st Garrison and the 
342nd Garrison trained well and improved in quality.  86 gold was expended 
to maintain our armies.  Uga-Buga Makumb was left with 45 gold in the 
treasury.


LETTER REPORTS

  O King of Kings, thy heroes have travelled around the globe in
search of adventure and conquest.  I have faithfully kept their
correspondence, and now I present it to thee:


From: Guerin
To: Uga-Buga Makumb

  Honored Sovereign, 

  In spring, I cast a Part Sea spell and the waters of Mercia were magically 
parted.
  Windy spring was followed by early summer.  Guerin's Part Sea spell 
backfired and I was left with my face covered with psycho-kinetic slime.
  With the advent of the late summer season, I marched my forces into Mercia 
(#1304).  Northeast of here is Quantara Roo (#1403), a Plain area ruled by 
Kahn, where Humans dwell.  Their Human warriors prefer to fight in the style 
of Legion and Heavy Cavalry.

  A conquering horde owing their allegiance to 
Shaman Uga-Buga Makumb Stuffy doll invaded the province of Mercia, a Plain 
area ruled by the provincial Whale chieftain.  I commanded the invading 
armies.  The defending forces were commanded by the provincial Whale 
chieftain.  The defending whales dove through the seawalls of the parted 
waters and flopped into their lines.  The armies were drawn up into lines of 
battle as shown here:

